<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lý Thái Tổ</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">974–1028</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Lý Công Uẩn, Ly Cong Uan, Ly Thai To</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lý Thái Tổ, born Lý Công Uẩn, was the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s Lý dynasty. Raised in a Buddhist monastery, he became a palace commander and was chosen as king in 1009 after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-long-dinh">Lê Long Đĩnh</a>, the last Early Lê ruler. In 1010 he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-lu">Hoa Lư</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-la">Đại La</a>, renaming it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>. He reigned until 1028.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ly-thai-to.jpg" alt="Lý Thái Tổ" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Whyte from Dublin, Ireland · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C6%B0%E1%BB%A3ng_L%C3%BD_Th%C3%A1i_T%E1%BB%95_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
